<BaseLayout
  title="Rapid Test vs PCR — Differences in Speed, Sensitivity & Use | Progene Bio"
  description="Rapid antigen/antibody test vs PCR (molecular) — how they differ in principle, speed, sensitivity and when each is appropriate."
  canonical="/comparisons/rapid-test-vs-pcr/"
>

  <h1>Rapid Test vs PCR</h1>

  <div class="card">
    <table>
      <thead><tr><th></th><th>Rapid test (lateral flow)</th><th>PCR (molecular)</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><th>Target</th><td>Antigen or antibody</td><td>Nucleic acid (RNA/DNA)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Time to result</th><td>Minutes (3–20)</td><td>Hours (lab) or ~30–60 min (POC)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Where</th><td>Point of care, no instrument</td><td>Laboratory / instrument</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Sensitivity</th><td>Lower (triage-grade)</td><td>Higher (gold standard)</td></tr>
        <tr><th>Cost</th><td>Lower</td><td>Higher</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>

  <h2>When to use each</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Rapid test:</strong> screening, triage, resource-limited settings, rapid public-health surveys.</li>
    <li><strong>PCR:</strong> confirmation, low-viral-load cases, and where high sensitivity is required.</li>
    <li>A negative rapid test during high clinical suspicion should be confirmed by molecular testing.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>Rapid tests are a triage aid, not a replacement for confirmatory laboratory testing.</p>

</BaseLayout>
